#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110419 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110790 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e2f2884-829b-5e09-8fc0-16556b177732" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Congruent Polygons on a Grid"}] :question/id #uuid "aedb6e30-d1e6-5d89-aa83-56c1d6946b50" :question/math-academy-id "e-2904" :question/problem "Are the polygons $\\mathcal{P}$ and $\\mathcal{Q}$ below congruent?\n\n![](images/75/75684fc94e43e8854403d543019f28f971b8b6adc0ca3f40cb673dc9dec6dcb9.png)" :question/worked-solution "In general, congruent polygons have the same shape. In other words:\n\n- they have the same number of sides,\n\n- all corresponding sides are congruent, and\n\n- all corresponding interior angles are congruent.\n\n$\\mathcal{P}$ and $\\mathcal{Q}$ have the same shape: They have congruent corresponding sides and congruent corresponding angles. This implies that $\\mathcal{P} \\cong \\mathcal{Q}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110420 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110791 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30fc8daf-a6eb-537b-b19b-06ad5504b5e6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Congruent Polygons Given Side and Angle Measures"}] :question/id #uuid "fd18dfda-f385-5e24-a200-882a71d3b063" :question/math-academy-id "e-2908" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true for the polygons $\\mathcal{A},$ $\\mathcal{B},$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ shown below?\n\nI. $\\mathcal{A}\\cong\\mathcal{B}$\n\nII. $\\mathcal{B}\\cong\\mathcal{C}$\n\nIII. $\\mathcal{C}\\cong\\mathcal{A}$\n\n![](images/a1/a13f283489521fc43d73a5a128b79ff937dca760c8cea330706efe6f39ac26f6.png)" :question/worked-solution "In general, congruent polygons have the same shape. In other words:\n\n- they have the same number of sides,\n\n- all corresponding sides are congruent, and\n\n- all corresponding interior angles are congruent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's compare the given polygons.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since the polygons $\\mathcal{A}$ and $\\mathcal{B}$ have the same number of sides and all corresponding sides and angles are congruent, we conclude that $\\mathcal{A}\\cong\\mathcal{B}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that the polygons $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ have the same number of sides, but they don't seem to have the same shape. In particular, we notice they don't have corresponding congruent angles ($\\mathcal{B}$ does not have right angles, while $\\mathcal{C}$ does).\n\n- Statement III is false. By the same reasoning as above, we can see that the polygons $\\mathcal{C}$ and $\\mathcal{A}$ are not congruent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110421 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110792 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "46dbecc9-70e8-55c9-b7d7-7b5629ea1549" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Congruence Statement"}] :question/id #uuid "b3ec8468-a64a-5f55-b392-8f0072fbdd78" :question/math-academy-id "e-2910" :question/problem "![](images/de/de9be6ab7fe0608003c7a0493b8aab19df0dd4353b085330fc2a8f8aea84e527.png)\n\nWrite down a correct congruence statement for the quadrilaterals shown in the diagram above." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $\\angle A \\cong \\angle C.$ \n\nLet's determine the vertices in the second quadrilateral that correspond to $A.$ First, notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =XY \\\\ AD & =XW \\\\ ∠A & ≅∠X\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n- vertex $A$ corresponds to vertex $X,$ and\n\n- vertex $C$ corresponds to vertex $Z$ (since $C$ is opposite $A$ and $Z$ is opposite $X$).\n\nAs for the other two vertices, notice we have two more pairs of congruent angles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∠B & ≅∠Y \\\\ ∠D & ≅∠W.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have two more pairs of congruent sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CD & =ZW \\\\ BC & =YZ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n- vertex $B$ corresponds to $Y,$ and\n\n- vertex $D$ corresponds to $W.$\n\nFinally, we conclude that a correct congruence statement is\n\n$$\nABCD \\cong XYZW .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110422 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110793 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0921774a-ff9d-5760-93eb-13517043d509" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Unknown Quantities From Congruent Polygons"}] :question/id #uuid "f6e8ab4f-f21a-5c5e-9682-9f9b00e4fdc1" :question/math-academy-id "e-2911" :question/problem "Given that $ABCDE \\cong PQRST,$ find $x+y.$\n\n![](images/88/881eaba27f633ffe94791fef3d3ebfd68504fa887796ec235db2c8546e160657.png)" :question/worked-solution "The statement $ABCDE \\cong PQRST$ means that $\\mathcal{K}$ and $\\mathcal{M}$ are congruent. Moreover,\n\n- vertex $A$ corresponds to vertex $P,$\n\n- vertex $B$ corresponds to vertex $Q,$\n\n- vertex $C$ corresponds to vertex $R,$\n\n- vertex $D$ corresponds to vertex $S,$\n\n- vertex $E$ corresponds to vertex $T.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CD & =RS \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A & =m∠P \\\\ {82}^{∘} & =(11y+5)^{∘} \\\\ 77 & =11y \\\\ 7 & =y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nx+y = 2+7=9.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499475 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110790 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e2f2884-829b-5e09-8fc0-16556b177732" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Congruent Polygons on a Grid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07eb3773-2b06-5f07-8768-565d885731ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/91/91ad731f4898c8a2c7ec06d7251ba05e79f62c47a4d3d0f793650d630010469c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498744} {:answer/id #uuid "37231f3e-5d81-5c45-bc71-2d5350fd20c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/35/351b8e99ed55ae97b9247d76ac95db363625610cc4051e1ef67492c499883315.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498852} {:answer/id #uuid "5dbb9840-97df-5822-805b-902c6a2faf76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c6/c69e44c755c2e7b3cd360c5bf29f23a895301997f5194d2596ef138063a864cd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498945} {:answer/id #uuid "cace4ddd-7b5c-58ef-bbf1-51c0b4a1260c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c2/c21fdd017ffe7340c3d60ae1b32c573a1a4cba39eaee52b1fd2c959f8fb13ea3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499176} {:answer/id #uuid "dba997b9-ff53-5809-9362-c10ccae21c0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/04/04ce6543e31c8dcf3809d0b09fd49be97e20025b59d3316c172c0a294b8122b9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499218}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07eb3773-2b06-5f07-8768-565d885731ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/91/91ad731f4898c8a2c7ec06d7251ba05e79f62c47a4d3d0f793650d630010469c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498744} :answer-field/id #uuid "0736d835-26b7-5aaa-a1d3-396416cf7db3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499311}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5a54090b-41ad-5e7f-a026-590e30e34f07" :question/math-academy-id "q-120128" :question/problem "![](images/a0/a09bde30c164f4ca5bf964690e734057efe0344f1136c611d04492a85cc8d4bb.png)\n\nGiven the polygon $P$ shown above, which of the following polygons is congruent to $P?$" :question/worked-solution "In general, congruent polygons have the same shape. In other words:\n\n- they have the same number of sides,\n\n- all corresponding sides are congruent, and\n\n- all corresponding interior angles are congruent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's compare each of the given polygons to $P.$\n\n- The polygon $P$ is a triangle (it has $3$ sides). So, the polygons $R$ and $S$ can't be congruent to the polygon $P$ because they do not have $3$ sides.\n\n- The polygons $U$ and  $Q$ are *not* congruent to $P$ (even though they both have $3$ sides) because $U$ and $Q$ don't have the same shape with $P.$\n\nOn the other hand, $P$ and $T$ have the same shape: They have congruent corresponding sides and congruent corresponding angles. This implies that $P≅T.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111065 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110790 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e2f2884-829b-5e09-8fc0-16556b177732" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Congruent Polygons on a Grid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bdda2ae4-764d-52e7-9586-f498c1b6c1a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8b/8b08ea40b8d23918440847ca2575c8f605d05c92b47972b06003f51caacf3da0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108148} {:answer/id #uuid "bf7b1d17-aec2-57c9-9aac-4ce9872dd490" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4f/4f1d670db55d76164853ba9ed242adf1f66f9d6149733cd79a70eda463b6de40.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108149} {:answer/id #uuid "02d35653-e633-50c6-8e75-4ccc00005561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9c/9c15c3e3c770fa4882310ee6fdfe8422ece90e0890b9134aa70ddeb2fcdd622a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108150} {:answer/id #uuid "3313235c-b672-53ac-9e87-b5f7c46136ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f6/f6b7f313f3981c6b86001598eaca47755078868b4a64245653514f23945f6e7a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108151} {:answer/id #uuid "58803afe-7a3f-547e-be53-9ae44e89b52a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/94/9404da2b8387019d762f885d06e7a7c994042e7ecb9a5183c8f7e293e932b8cb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108152}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58803afe-7a3f-547e-be53-9ae44e89b52a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/94/9404da2b8387019d762f885d06e7a7c994042e7ecb9a5183c8f7e293e932b8cb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108152} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c68b1cc-0709-544e-9068-1dee7271deb7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108147}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "baa02d67-eb00-5704-8c7d-807db27a6b6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-25866" :question/problem "![](images/10/10cd6e85765f6b79ab4bdab95f9ba9c34b012529b9bcd5d3cbdb93765cd99d6a.png)\n\nGiven the polygon $P$ shown above, which of the following polygons is congruent to $P?$" :question/worked-solution "In general, congruent polygons have the same shape. In other words:\n\n- they have the same number of sides,\n\n- all corresponding sides are congruent, and\n\n- all corresponding interior angles are congruent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's compare each of the given polygons to $P.$\n\n- The polygon $P$ is an octagon (it has eight sides). So, the polygons $R,$ $T,$ and $Q$ can't be congruent to the polygon $P$ because they do not have $8$ sides.\n\n- The polygon $S$ is *not* congruent to $\\,P$ (even though they both have $8$ sides) because $S$ and $P$ don't have the same shape. In particular, notice that $P$ has a right angle, while $S$ doesn't have a right angle.\n\nOn the other hand, $P$ and $U$ have the same shape: They have congruent corresponding sides and congruent corresponding angles. This implies that $P≅U.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111093 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110791 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30fc8daf-a6eb-537b-b19b-06ad5504b5e6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Congruent Polygons Given Side and Angle Measures"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89c746ad-1610-5931-ab5c-45bb4f97b7a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108318} {:answer/id #uuid "934b17a8-94aa-5fb3-b0f6-3650cb25a4f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108319} {:answer/id #uuid "c870e598-a227-564b-a0a9-1e23e5654d6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108320} {:answer/id #uuid "028ecd1f-f636-5e81-8aaf-126986c2bd32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108321} {:answer/id #uuid "74ca8c5b-bfb1-58ef-b546-5092219f4348" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108322}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74ca8c5b-bfb1-58ef-b546-5092219f4348" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108322} :answer-field/id #uuid "38e76745-24d5-5762-8d5c-0a948cb43c06" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108317}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "de8441f9-b5b3-54ab-a25c-529c00dca814" :question/math-academy-id "q-26504" :question/problem "![](images/fa/fa57e1d088e5384cfd7a08d524877e6a9fafce413f0b3039d59d90dd074d361b.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true for the polygons $P$, $Q$ and $R$ shown above?\n\nI. $P≅Q$\n\nII. $Q≅R$\n\nIII. $P≅R$" :question/worked-solution "In general, congruent polygons have the same shape. In other words:\n\n- they have the same number of sides,\n\n- all corresponding sides are congruent, and\n\n- all corresponding interior angles are congruent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's compare the given polygons.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that the polygons $P$ and $Q$ have the same number of sides, but they don't seem to have the same shape. In particular, we notice they don't have corresponding congruent angles ($P$ does not have right angles, while $Q$ does).\n\n- Statement II is false. By the same reasoning as above, we can see that the polygons $Q$ and $R$ are not congruent.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the polygons $P$ and $R$ have the same number of sides and all corresponding sides and angles are congruent, we conclude that $P≅R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499471 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110793 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0921774a-ff9d-5760-93eb-13517043d509" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Unknown Quantities From Congruent Polygons"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18ff3e53-c48a-516e-a24d-a516a1cebf3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498783} {:answer/id #uuid "6577f21e-3ae4-5455-a348-ad44a3d54183" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498956} {:answer/id #uuid "bc92ce30-fda0-5783-957a-cda20911e5ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499149} {:answer/id #uuid "d9192bc3-956c-5822-9b42-21395c0ed1a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499214} {:answer/id #uuid "e0977b69-9c0f-5940-9836-f360fd82e031" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499231}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6577f21e-3ae4-5455-a348-ad44a3d54183" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498956} :answer-field/id #uuid "aad6a91e-f93b-5ecf-b16f-9fda253dd13c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499394}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "56adf5b0-549f-5613-9c35-0245d2557d7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-53484" :question/problem "![](images/d4/d46182f3d4373a6b8bb1d330afb51a4ed2eea0c33b2f929b5538d7e0edf76353.png)\n\nGiven that $ABCDE≅MPQRT,$ find $x-2y.$" :question/worked-solution "The statement $ABCDE≅MPQRT$ means that $P$ and $Q$ are congruent. Moreover,\n\n- vertex $A$ corresponds to vertex $M,$\n\n- vertex $B$ corresponds to vertex $P,$\n\n- vertex $C$ corresponds to vertex $Q,$\n\n- vertex $D$ corresponds to vertex $R,$\n\n- vertex $E$ corresponds to vertex $T.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}MP & =AB \\\\ y-4 & =2 \\\\ y & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠E & =m∠T \\\\ {(3x+20)}^{∘} & ={140}^{∘} \\\\ 3x+20 & =140 \\\\ 3x & =120 \\\\ x & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nx-2y=40-12=28.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499493 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110790 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e2f2884-829b-5e09-8fc0-16556b177732" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Congruent Polygons on a Grid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5999cbad-3533-5a28-86bc-7105ff649c23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c7/c70d748e361974703e32f71d5a5bdf3331d307604daddff6bfcfc76554247ada.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498935} {:answer/id #uuid "751dfdb5-e83c-53c6-901d-708dbb902b04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6f/6fda52b861c386e5806f332fc196b45301a303ee4143b620ec6b4d59f9709ae8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498990} {:answer/id #uuid "91fd0b02-269d-52eb-bc14-61c1379c9385" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/79/79808a9159326e773d646f195cf31c0a308fa84665397547aef3a0a528b823d2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499048} {:answer/id #uuid "acf3d2f0-5ba7-52a7-9a17-a5f962577931" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/68/6878bc99517b9dce633c4c4d4787fa4baf10abee323464cfc5053c775d7a4c73.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499111} {:answer/id #uuid "ecf98f27-4f06-5351-a6c8-812705f5db11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/25/25bc3b7f9b41984e072f6390b0175ad2fc8f428068be408d5051859b93090095.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499263}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "acf3d2f0-5ba7-52a7-9a17-a5f962577931" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/68/6878bc99517b9dce633c4c4d4787fa4baf10abee323464cfc5053c775d7a4c73.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499111} :answer-field/id #uuid "4dc837ad-cec2-516e-abf6-8e7e695d7692" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7ca08efa-e8ff-5fd7-8af8-59ae906150bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-87636" :question/problem "![](images/81/81c7a4f9f0b9ab855b22ad7895f5f0d7b1ae3711563e9691117dfd99f878dccb.png)\n\nGiven the polygon $P$ shown above, which of the following polygons is congruent to $P?$" :question/worked-solution "In general, congruent polygons have the same shape. In other words:\n\n- they have the same number of sides,\n\n- all corresponding sides are congruent, and\n\n- all corresponding interior angles are congruent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's compare each of the given polygons to $P.$\n\n- The polygon $P$ is a pentagon (it has $5$ sides). So, the polygons $S,$ $T,$ and $U$ can't be congruent to the polygon $P$ because they do not have $5$ sides.\n\n- The polygon $Q$ is *not* congruent to $\\,P$ (even though they both have $5$ sides) because $Q$ and $P$ don't have the same shape. In particular, notice that $P$ has two right angles, while $Q$ doesn't have right angles.\n\nOn the other hand, $P$ and $R$ have the same shape: They have congruent corresponding sides and congruent corresponding angles. This implies that $P≅R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499487 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110791 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30fc8daf-a6eb-537b-b19b-06ad5504b5e6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Congruent Polygons Given Side and Angle Measures"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58edac1f-feb0-54d4-b741-91efad1f1ea5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498933} {:answer/id #uuid "857013b4-977e-536e-ac4e-e30af5e43ebb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499031} {:answer/id #uuid "9989a249-cc1f-5748-8610-34cdd6aee401" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499058} {:answer/id #uuid "c7f85715-4769-5d49-bc83-9e01f63db4c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499167} {:answer/id #uuid "f639202d-30f9-52e5-bacd-15e0ee2bbd38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f639202d-30f9-52e5-bacd-15e0ee2bbd38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499279} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d3a6dd2-15b7-5de9-ae4d-95d7d271e950" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6b6253c5-dbcb-5eca-bada-e65b2d371ca0" :question/math-academy-id "q-87683" :question/problem "![](images/23/2375302a1591f251e33b1d3813e8ea500ce0f7cb85082513d67c30e09e5b8670.png)\n\nGiven the polygons shown above, which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $C≅V$\n\nII. $V≅B$\n\nIII. $B≅C$" :question/worked-solution "In general, congruent polygons have the same shape. In other words:\n\n- they have the same number of sides,\n\n- all corresponding sides are congruent, and\n\n- all corresponding interior angles are congruent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's compare the given polygons.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that the polygons $C$ and $V$ have the same number of sides, but they don't seem to have the same shape. In particular, we notice they don't have corresponding congruent sides.\n\n- Statement II is false. By the same reasoning as statement I, we can see that the polygons $V$ and $B$ are not congruent.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the polygons $B$ and $C$ have the same number of sides and all corresponding sides and angles are congruent, we conclude that $B≅C.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499557 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110792 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "46dbecc9-70e8-55c9-b7d7-7b5629ea1549" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Congruence Statement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "479bbce0-b19a-5087-8d81-83489d89c8fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "RSTU≅FEHG" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498894} {:answer/id #uuid "4e38c389-29b6-571c-bb38-fc6b05b64f2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "RSTU≅HEFG" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498911} {:answer/id #uuid "8727bf22-7447-5ffc-974a-916e5c2c7d22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "RSTU≅HGFE" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499033} {:answer/id #uuid "a2284bec-f4c6-517d-837b-49a74b01b5d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "RSTU≅EFGH" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499077} {:answer/id #uuid "e23c45f0-023d-56cd-bfe9-f37032cdbb30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "RSTU≅FGHE" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499240}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "479bbce0-b19a-5087-8d81-83489d89c8fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "RSTU≅FEHG" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498894} :answer-field/id #uuid "d56a4bca-54d6-5320-a849-6ac02a4b2246" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499413}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ef93482b-ea08-53c2-acb8-85ef03af9f1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-87759" :question/problem "![](images/56/56c2afca682997fa2ead3f4962d5de4e347d99164e257737f68ddafea385ae4f.png)\n\nWhat is a correct congruence statement for the quadrilaterals shown in the diagram above?" :question/worked-solution "We have four pairs of congruent angles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∠R & ≅∠F \\\\ ∠S & ≅∠E \\\\ ∠T & ≅∠H \\\\ ∠U & ≅∠G.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have four pairs of congruent sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RS & =FE \\\\ ST & =EH \\\\ TU & =HG \\\\ UR & =GF.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince all corresponding sides and angles are congruent, the two quadrilaterals are congruent. In particular\n\n- vertex $R$ corresponds to vertex $F,$\n\n- vertex $S$ corresponds to vertex $E,$\n\n- vertex $T$ corresponds to vertex $H,$ and\n\n- vertex $U$ corresponds to vertex $G.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that a correct congruence statement is\n\n$$\nRSTU≅FEHG.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499533 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110792 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "46dbecc9-70e8-55c9-b7d7-7b5629ea1549" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Congruence Statement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03aa5cc4-4b95-57f8-b771-fadacf80d20b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "MNOP≅GFED" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498737} {:answer/id #uuid "6f44188c-f972-57f3-b7e7-e9fe02835e26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "MNOP≅FGDE" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498976} {:answer/id #uuid "8187b21f-e02b-53e8-b9d2-7a643b373f5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "MNOP≅DEFG" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499020} {:answer/id #uuid "a6ba2fb0-b878-5bee-82a7-fa68d5315da6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "MNOP≅GDEF" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499096} {:answer/id #uuid "fd9afeae-e1f2-5c31-94da-d159496f0b8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "MNOP≅DGFE" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499300}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8187b21f-e02b-53e8-b9d2-7a643b373f5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "MNOP≅DEFG" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499020} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a04f70f-7747-529e-a1eb-8502c8e8a0b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499377}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c4dcd0bc-d6f0-59ef-aa66-febcad580925" :question/math-academy-id "q-87760" :question/problem "![](images/7c/7c1663caeea9751ccbaa2544792c9c15331accedc6dd757b44154673f34f8ddc.png)\n\nWhat is a correct congruence statement for the quadrilaterals shown in the diagram above?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $∠M≅∠O.$ \n\nLet's determine the vertices in the second quadrilateral that correspond to $M.$ First, notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}MN & =DE \\\\ MP & =DG \\\\ ∠M & ≅∠D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n- vertex $M$ corresponds to vertex $D,$\n\n- consequently, vertex $O$ corresponds to vertex $F.$\n\nAs for the other two vertices, notice we have two more pairs of congruent angles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∠N & ≅∠E \\\\ ∠P & ≅∠G.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have two more pairs of congruent sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}OP & =FG \\\\ ON & =FE,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n- vertex $N$ corresponds to $E,$ and\n\n- vertex $P$ corresponds to $G.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that a correct congruence statement is\n\n$$\nMNOP≅DEFG.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499465 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110792 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "46dbecc9-70e8-55c9-b7d7-7b5629ea1549" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Congruence Statement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40d3b24e-0a5a-5141-95ab-7273b1a3d969" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "VWXYZ≅JNMLK" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498879} {:answer/id #uuid "48ff17ea-cfb8-5afc-9053-fee10e41317d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "VWXYZ≅LMNJK" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498898} {:answer/id #uuid "547bc0c7-678a-5654-a74d-713c28dbc89a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "VWXYZ≅KLMNJ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498923} {:answer/id #uuid "704b85ff-0640-5bbc-9822-6e64cb927711" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "VWXYZ≅JKLMN" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498981} {:answer/id #uuid "f254091b-5072-546b-9701-5bc75a8899b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "VWXYZ≅KJNML" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499273}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "704b85ff-0640-5bbc-9822-6e64cb927711" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "VWXYZ≅JKLMN" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498981} :answer-field/id #uuid "7807d3b5-5268-5991-924a-d2c5e3a72655" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499376}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "486306f3-8fb2-5f6e-b7bd-26ff06793aaa" :question/math-academy-id "q-87761" :question/problem "![](images/9a/9a4182f96ede84fdae27964aa21fc0537a37e279ec7eb7c390aeb94d1cae8eb9.png)\n\nWhat is a correct congruence statement for the pentagons shown in the diagram above?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $∠W≅∠Z.$ \n\nLet's determine the vertices in the second pentagon that correspond to $Z.$ First, notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}YZ & =MN \\\\ ZV & =NJ \\\\ ∠Z & ≅∠N\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n- vertex $Z$ corresponds to vertex $N,$\n\n- consequently, vertex $W$ corresponds to vertex $K.$\n\nAs for the other three vertices, notice we have two more pairs of congruent angles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∠X & ≅∠L \\\\ ∠Y & ≅∠M.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have two more pairs of congruent sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =ML \\\\ WX & =LK.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n- vertex $X$ corresponds to vertex $L,$\n\n- vertex $Y$ corresponds to vertex $M,$ and\n\n- vertex $V$ corresponds to vertex $J$ because they both correspond to the right angle.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that a correct congruence statement is\n\n$$\nVWXYZ≅JKLMN.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499452 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110793 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0921774a-ff9d-5760-93eb-13517043d509" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Unknown Quantities From Congruent Polygons"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21d7f618-d14e-56fe-a6e3-73a4572994a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+5\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498805} {:answer/id #uuid "495ca8d8-22a7-5291-ae63-d43fdb6217e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498899} {:answer/id #uuid "5ed0a6a0-c987-52cf-aeed-6999ca315df1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498949} {:answer/id #uuid "93231cb6-dff5-5394-bf8b-bccb088fc470" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499049} {:answer/id #uuid "b94ca3d6-7062-5e6c-b257-028d0396c9bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5(2+\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499145}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b94ca3d6-7062-5e6c-b257-028d0396c9bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5(2+\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499145} :answer-field/id #uuid "c892b43d-4540-59a6-b73b-33d123922a45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499406}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "25b1bc91-494d-53b8-abed-15f163bc2377" :question/math-academy-id "q-87769" :question/problem "![](images/c1/c13c5ac52b7bc508032d2d53b1169730641bafefcd8c0f5487d96699d6120946.png)\n\nConsider the triangles $P$ and $Q$ shown above. Given that $WXY≅FGH,$ what is the perimeter of $Q?$" :question/worked-solution "The statement $WXY≅FGH$ means that $P$ and $Q$ are congruent. Moreover,\n\n- vertex $W$ corresponds to vertex $F,$\n\n- vertex $X$ corresponds to vertex $G,$\n\n- vertex $Y$ corresponds to vertex $H.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}FG & =WX \\\\ t & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}HF & =YW \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\,t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe figure shows that $FG=GH.$ Therefore, the perimeter of $Q$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}_{Q} & =FG+GH+HF \\\\  & =5+5+5\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =5(2+\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
